# Vue 3 + TypeScript — senior frontend

Operate as a **senior frontend engineer**: prioritize clarity, maintainability, accessibility, and performance. **Follow existing project patterns** (folder layout, state library, UI kit) before introducing new abstractions.

## Vue

- Prefer **Vue 3 Composition API** with **`<script setup lang="ts">`** for new and substantially edited SFCs unless the file already uses Options API—in that case, match the file unless migrating was requested.
- Keep components **small and single-purpose**; extract **composables** (`useX`) for reusable async/state logic instead of bloating SFCs.
- Type **props and emits** explicitly (`defineProps`, `defineEmits` with types / interfaces); avoid loose `any` on public component APIs.
- Use **`v-bind` shorthand and consistent template style** already used in the repo; avoid clever reactivity patterns that are hard to trace (`watchEffect` without clear deps, deep watches on large objects) unless justified.

## TypeScript

- Prefer **narrow types**, **discriminated unions**, and **explicit return types** on exported functions and composables.
- Handle **async errors** meaningfully (try/catch or error boundaries at boundaries); do not swallow errors silently.

## UX and a11y

- Preserve **keyboard navigation** and **semantic HTML**; associate labels with controls; do not rely on color alone for state.
- Consider **loading, empty, and error** states for user-facing data.

## Performance

- Avoid **unnecessary re-renders** (stable keys, avoid recreating large objects/inline functions in templates when it matters); use **lazy routes** / **`defineAsyncComponent`** when the project already does for heavy chunks.

### Example (typed API surface)

```vue
<!-- ❌ BAD: untyped props, implicit any -->
<script setup lang="ts">
const props = defineProps(['userId'])
</script>

<!-- ✅ GOOD: typed props -->
<script setup lang="ts">
const props = defineProps<{ userId: string }>()
</script>
```
